WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:07.220
Hi, my name is Benjy
Braun and I'm really

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:09.740
excited to talk to you
today about getting data

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:12.080
science in your
customers' hands quickly

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:14.760
and on a budget using
RStudio Connect.

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:17.060
This is an unconventional
use of this product,

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:20.490
which is really geared towards
internal apps and resources.

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:22.070
But I want to show
you how you can

00:00:22.070 --> 00:00:25.520
use this product for customer
facing applications as well

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:26.840
as internal ones.

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:28.790
But before I get
into that, I wanted

00:00:28.790 --> 00:00:30.680
to set the scene
for how we started

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:34.130
using RStudio Connect to publish
customer facing data science

00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:35.160
applications.

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:41.290
It was back in the
summer of 2020.

00:00:41.290 --> 00:00:43.140
We all remember,
but maybe we'd like

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:45.390
to forget that time
in addition to being

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:48.700
in the middle of a lockdown
and without childcare,

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:50.880
my family and I had
moved in with my in-laws

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:52.530
to get some extra support.

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:56.070
Moreover, my wife had gone full
time on her own new business

00:00:56.070 --> 00:00:58.320
back in January
2020, while I had

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:01.830
joined a startup as the first
technical employee in February

00:01:01.830 --> 00:01:02.580
2020.

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:06.000
Needless to say, it
was a stressful time.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.100
I have a feeling this
is a picture many of you

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:10.420
with kids have or at
least can relate to.

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:12.930
If you don't have kids,
just imagine everything

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:15.250
you're doing back
in summer 2020.

00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:17.190
But add a two-year-old
and a four-year-old

00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:19.540
to the mix as all
this was going on.

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:23.910
My company, 202 Group, was
dealing with a problem.

00:01:23.910 --> 00:01:26.740
We had built a good,
streamlined data pipeline.

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:28.360
We had sourced necessary data

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:30.930
we needed to make actionable
insights for our customers

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:33.850
and built the methods to
ingest and store that data.

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:36.150
We had effective ways
to analyze and model

00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:38.490
the data and customize
visualizations for

00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:39.580
end customers.

00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:41.950
We were using open
data and data science

00:01:41.950 --> 00:01:44.940
to expose risk in critical
national security supply

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:47.680
chains, and our customers
were clamoring for more

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:49.380
so they could take action.

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:52.530
But despite this tested
problem, this tested process,

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:54.840
we had a problem.

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:58.840
We had a really poor UI/UX
if you could even call it that.

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:03.150
We were delivering everything in
PDFs, PowerPoint and Excel CSV

00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:04.830
files.

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:07.240
This is a big problem
for two reasons.

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:09.990
The first is that once
PowerPoints or PDFs

00:02:09.990 --> 00:02:13.690
or spreadsheets left our inbox,
it was immediately out of date.

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:16.140
The customer could still get
value out of it, but they

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:18.720
relied on us emailing
them updates, which were

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:20.410
out of date almost immediately.

00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:24.250
They needed an on
demand up to date data.

00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:26.280
The second problem
was that it wasn't

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:27.810
an appealing way
for the customer

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:30.090
to consume the analysis.

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:32.580
At first you might not think
that's such a big problem,

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:34.080
but if your customer
doesn't like

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:37.620
the way they interact with your
product, they won't use it.

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:39.630
So if you actually
want your analysis

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:41.910
to be anything more
than a science project,

00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:43.770
you need to make sure
your customer likes

00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:45.360
the way they interact with it.

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:48.240
And for us as a small
bootstrap startup,

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:51.082
our customer liking the product
meant still being in business

00:02:51.082 --> 00:02:52.040
by the end of the year.

00:02:54.870 --> 00:02:57.840
The customer needed to be able
to explore on their own

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:00.810
in a user friendly way and
get the data into formats

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:02.370
that they were comfortable with.

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:05.340
It needed to be dynamic
to fix the stale data

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:09.650
problem and the static
interface problem.

00:03:09.650 --> 00:03:11.740
But we had other
requirements as well.

00:03:11.740 --> 00:03:14.750
We needed to get this out the door
quickly in the next few weeks,

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:15.740
not months.

00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:18.830
And we didn't have very
much money to do it.

00:03:18.830 --> 00:03:22.010
Last but certainly not
least, we needed something

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:24.440
where we could continue to
focus on the data science

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:25.860
portion of our company.

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:27.830
The thing that was
differentiating us,

00:03:27.830 --> 00:03:30.770
the value we were actually
delivering to customers

00:03:30.770 --> 00:03:34.400
was the data science finding,
not the interface itself.

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:36.500
Learning web
development technologies

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:39.650
like CSS, HTML, JavaScript.

00:03:39.650 --> 00:03:42.050
PHP would not only
be a distraction,

00:03:42.050 --> 00:03:44.690
it would actively detract
from our time developing

00:03:44.690 --> 00:03:47.635
the data science, which is where
we were really driving value.

00:03:50.350 --> 00:03:52.690
So based on all this,
we developed a set

00:03:52.690 --> 00:03:55.960
of five criteria that we would
use to evaluate the proposed

00:03:55.960 --> 00:03:59.080
solutions to this problem of
getting up to date data science

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:01.230
products in our
customer's hands.

00:04:01.230 --> 00:04:04.420
It had to be cheap, like
a few thousand dollars tops.

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:06.120
It had to be easy to use.

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:08.590
We needed to be able to
focus on the data science

00:04:08.590 --> 00:04:11.830
and not spend a lot of time
figuring out web technologies.

00:04:11.830 --> 00:04:13.940
It had to be easy to customize.

00:04:13.940 --> 00:04:17.050
Our customers were still
figuring out what they wanted

00:04:17.050 --> 00:04:19.570
and we needed to be able
to adapt and make changes

00:04:19.570 --> 00:04:20.810
quickly and easily.

00:04:20.810 --> 00:04:23.110
The user had to log
into an interface

00:04:23.110 --> 00:04:26.230
the customer needed to be
able to access up to date info

00:04:26.230 --> 00:04:27.710
when they needed it.

00:04:27.710 --> 00:04:30.820
Emailing static reports
had to end, and last

00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:32.660
we had to retain our IP.

00:04:32.660 --> 00:04:35.660
Getting locked in with a vendor
didn't seem like a good idea.

00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:37.900
And we wanted to maintain
flexibility in the future

00:04:37.900 --> 00:04:39.405
if we started doing
something new.

00:04:42.530 --> 00:04:44.780
So we settled on three options.

00:04:44.780 --> 00:04:48.710
We considered third party BI
tools, like Tableau or Power

00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:52.840
BI, hiring a front end developer,
and last RStudio Connect.

00:04:55.420 --> 00:04:58.020
Some of my teammates were
pushing for this option.

00:04:58.020 --> 00:04:59.970
I was against it
for a few reasons.

00:04:59.970 --> 00:05:02.320
The startup costs are
actually pretty expensive,

00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:04.580
which is a big driver
for us at this point.

00:05:04.580 --> 00:05:07.640
But there are two other,
perhaps more important reasons.

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:10.720
One is that learning to
use one of these tools

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:12.620
was really a poor
use of our time.

00:05:12.620 --> 00:05:15.230
We already knew how to
use R Markdown and Shiny.

00:05:15.230 --> 00:05:16.990
Why learn how to use
one of these tools

00:05:16.990 --> 00:05:19.310
when we could be
developing data science?

00:05:19.310 --> 00:05:20.920
If we were going
to use these tools

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:22.630
and learn how to use
them from scratch,

00:05:22.630 --> 00:05:24.970
we might as well learn
JavaScript ourselves.

00:05:24.970 --> 00:05:27.520
These tools might market
themselves as easy to use,

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:29.440
but in practice I
don't think it's really

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:31.090
that easy, especially
if you want

00:05:31.090 --> 00:05:33.610
to produce something appealing.

00:05:33.610 --> 00:05:36.220
Second is that we'd get
locked into the vendor

00:05:36.220 --> 00:05:37.970
and switching costs
would be high.

00:05:37.970 --> 00:05:41.020
Anything we'd develop would
be specific to the vendor's

00:05:41.020 --> 00:05:43.330
product and we'd lose
flexibility if we

00:05:43.330 --> 00:05:46.100
wanted to do something else.

00:05:46.100 --> 00:05:48.850
So a third party BI tools
had two out of three.

00:05:48.850 --> 00:05:51.920
I was ready to move on.

00:05:51.920 --> 00:05:53.820
What about hiring for this need?

00:05:53.820 --> 00:05:55.370
The problem here
was that it wasn't

00:05:55.370 --> 00:05:57.320
going to be cheap
to get someone good.

00:05:57.320 --> 00:05:59.720
And while it might be easier
than doing it ourselves,

00:05:59.720 --> 00:06:01.590
it would be hard to
source someone good

00:06:01.590 --> 00:06:03.590
and then manage them
through the process.

00:06:03.590 --> 00:06:06.410
We also wanted to stay
focused on our value, which

00:06:06.410 --> 00:06:09.050
was in the data science and
hiring outside of that would

00:06:09.050 --> 00:06:11.000
also have been a distraction.

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:12.800
If we were going
to hire, we'd want

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:17.600
to hire another data
scientist or engineer.

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:20.090
So better than the third
party tools, but still

00:06:20.090 --> 00:06:22.490
only three out of five.

00:06:22.490 --> 00:06:26.030
So this left us with
RStudio Connect.

00:06:26.030 --> 00:06:28.070
I was only familiar
with this product

00:06:28.070 --> 00:06:30.950
because I had goofed around
enough in the RStudio IDE

00:06:30.950 --> 00:06:35.358
and clicked on this button
here the Publish button.

00:06:35.358 --> 00:06:37.400
This was really helpful
when I published the blog

00:06:37.400 --> 00:06:38.460
using RPubss.

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:41.180
So the idea of a button
in the RStudio IDE

00:06:41.180 --> 00:06:44.000
to publish R Markdown
and share them securely

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:46.760
was really appealing.

00:06:46.760 --> 00:06:48.500
Then when we got in
touch with RStudio

00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:50.670
with RStudio about
using Connect,

00:06:50.670 --> 00:06:52.990
I found out about the
small business discount.

00:06:52.990 --> 00:06:55.220
This made this option
far and away cheaper

00:06:55.220 --> 00:06:57.110
than the other
options we considered.

00:06:57.110 --> 00:06:59.330
And since we got to
keep using R Markdown,

00:06:59.330 --> 00:07:03.650
we didn't have vendor lock in
and we retained all our own IP.

00:07:03.650 --> 00:07:07.380
So checks across the board.

00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:10.450
It checked all the boxes, which
is why we use it going in.

00:07:10.450 --> 00:07:12.510
But the reason why we
continue to use it,

00:07:12.510 --> 00:07:15.670
and I'm such an evangelist
for it now, is what made it

00:07:15.670 --> 00:07:20.190
successful. We were able to change,
update and push content

00:07:20.190 --> 00:07:21.010
quickly.

00:07:21.010 --> 00:07:23.970
We could respond to customers,
both in terms of access

00:07:23.970 --> 00:07:25.690
and data within minutes.

00:07:25.690 --> 00:07:28.200
And as time went on,
we were iteratively

00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:30.690
able to learn more web
and front end technologies

00:07:30.690 --> 00:07:32.770
and incorporate them
into the end product.

00:07:32.770 --> 00:07:35.700
This included custom
CSS and JavaScript

00:07:35.700 --> 00:07:37.890
to give our products
a unique look and feel

00:07:37.890 --> 00:07:43.040
and better user experience with
only minimal front end needs.

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:45.590
So we've gotten to the point
based on customer needs

00:07:45.590 --> 00:07:47.190
and where we are as a company

00:07:47.190 --> 00:07:51.290
that we now do deliver a product
and a custom JavaScript PHP front

00:07:51.290 --> 00:07:53.840
end with a dedicated
front end team.

00:07:53.840 --> 00:07:56.660
And now we use RStudio Connect
for its more conventional

00:07:56.660 --> 00:07:59.130
purpose of hosting
internal products.

00:07:59.130 --> 00:08:01.520
But what's awesome is that
everything we developed

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:04.580
was in R Markdown, so
those rendered HTML docs

00:08:04.580 --> 00:08:09.780
and graphics could be used
by our front end developers.

00:08:09.780 --> 00:08:11.880
So I want you to
think about our story

00:08:11.880 --> 00:08:13.350
and how it might work for you.

00:08:13.350 --> 00:08:16.320
If you're a small business or
even an individual and you're

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:17.910
not sure how to get
your data science

00:08:17.910 --> 00:08:19.360
into your customers' hands,

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:20.940
think of RStudio Connect.

00:08:20.940 --> 00:08:23.610
It's cheap, easy to
use, easy to update

00:08:23.610 --> 00:08:26.310
your, update your products,
lets your customers

00:08:26.310 --> 00:08:28.590
log into an interface
and lets you retain all

00:08:28.590 --> 00:08:30.400
your IP without vendor lock in.

00:08:34.460 --> 00:08:36.110
Please feel free to
reach out to talk

00:08:36.110 --> 00:08:37.799
about this or anything else.

00:08:37.799 --> 00:08:41.410
Thank you so much for your time
and coming to hear my talk.

